/** @page sandboxing Sandboxing and Security

    @tableofcontents

    %Qore provides comprehensive sandboxing capabilities for safely executing untrusted or semi-trusted code.
    This is essential for applications that need to run user-provided code, such as:

    - Multi-tenant SaaS platforms
    - Plugin and extension systems
    - Online code execution environments
    - Web application scripting engines
    - Workflow and automation systems

    %Qore offers two complementary approaches to sandboxing:

    1. <b>Parse Options</b> - Binary on/off restrictions that completely disable categories of functionality
    2. <b>SandboxManager</b> - Fine-grained, configurable restrictions with allow/deny lists and resource limits

    @section sandbox_parse_options Parse Option Restrictions

    Parse options provide a simple way to completely disable access to certain system capabilities.
    These are enforced at parse time, meaning code that attempts to use restricted features will
    fail to parse rather than failing at runtime.

    @subsection sandbox_po_overview Overview of Security Parse Options

    The following parse options restrict system access:

    |!Parse Option|!Description
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM"|Disables all filesystem access
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK "PO_NO_NETWORK"|Disables all network access
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL"|Disables thread creation and control
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CLASSES "PO_NO_THREAD_CLASSES"|Disables thread synchronization classes
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREADS "PO_NO_THREADS"|Combines PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL and PO_NO_THREAD_CLASSES
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL "PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL"|Disables process control (exit, fork, etc.)
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS"|Disables spawning external processes
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_INFO "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_INFO"|Disables access to external system information
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS"|Combines multiple restrictions for external access
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_DATABASE "PO_NO_DATABASE"|Disables database access
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_MODULES "PO_NO_MODULES"|Disables loading modules
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_IO "PO_NO_IO"|Disables all I/O operations
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_GUI "PO_NO_GUI"|Disables GUI functionality
    |@ref Qore::PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS "PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS"|Disables APIs that could bypass sandboxing

    @subsection sandbox_po_usage Using Parse Options

    Parse options can be set when creating a @ref Qore::Program "Program" object:

    @code{.py}
%new-style
%require-types
%strict-args

# Create a program with no filesystem or network access
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE | PO_NO_FILESYSTEM | PO_NO_NETWORK);

# Parse user code - any filesystem/network calls will cause parse errors
try {
    p.parse(user_code, "user_script");
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    printf("Parse error: %s\n", ex.desc);
}
    @endcode

    Parse options can also be set via parse directives in the code itself:

    @code{.py}
%no-filesystem
%no-network

# This code cannot access files or network
    @endcode

    Or via command-line options:

    @verbatim
qore --no-filesystem --no-network script.qr
    @endverbatim

    @subsection sandbox_po_lockdown Lockdown Parse Option

    The @ref Qore::PO_LOCKDOWN "PO_LOCKDOWN" parse option combines many restrictions for maximum security:

    @code{.py}
# PO_LOCKDOWN includes:
# - PO_NO_PROCESS_CONTROL
# - PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL
# - PO_NO_TOP_LEVEL_STATEMENTS
# - PO_NO_GLOBAL_VARS
# - PO_NO_SUBROUTINE_DEFS
# - PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS (which includes PO_NO_FILESYSTEM, PO_NO_NETWORK, etc.)

Program p(PO_LOCKDOWN);
    @endcode

    For even stricter sandboxing, you can use the @ref Qore::ParseOptions "ParseOptions" class to add
    extended options (bits 64+) that go beyond the 64-bit \c int limitation:

    @code{.py}
ParseOptions opts(PO_LOCKDOWN);
opts.set("no-class-defs");      # prevent class definitions
opts.set("no-constant-defs");   # prevent constant definitions
opts.set("no-namespace-defs");  # prevent namespace definitions
opts.set("no-new");             # prevent use of the 'new' operator
Program p(opts);
    @endcode

    @see @ref Qore::ParseOptions "ParseOptions"

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @subsection sandbox_po_limitations Parse Option Limitations

    While parse options are effective for completely disabling functionality, they have limitations:

    - <b>Binary</b>: Cannot allow partial access (e.g., allow some files but not others)
    - <b>No resource limits</b>: Cannot limit the number of threads
    - <b>No interruption</b>: Cannot stop runaway code (infinite loops)
    - <b>No SSRF prevention</b>: Cannot block specific IP ranges
    - <b>The Program's own code</b>: @ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK "PO_NO_NETWORK" and
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM" restrict the code parsed in the %Program.  Module code that the
      %Program calls does not inherit them: a module that the host gave the %Program is a capability that the host
      granted it, and the network and filesystem access of module code, also on the I/O threads that run it, is
      governed by the %Program's @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager".  To keep a %Program and the modules it
      was given off the network or the filesystem, give it a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" with a
      default deny policy (see @ref sandbox_net_rules and @ref sandbox_fs_policy); the other restrictions of the
      %Program apply to the module code that it calls (see @ref user_module_runtime_context)
    - <b>Threads started by module code</b>: @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL" and the other
      thread parse options are checked when the code of the %Program is parsed, so they do not guarantee that the
      %Program cannot cause concurrent execution; see @ref sandbox_po_threads

    For these advanced use cases, use the @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" class.

    @subsection sandbox_po_threads Thread Parse Options and Module Code

    @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL" restricts the code of the %Program itself: its code cannot
    use the @ref background "background" operator, the @ref thread_exit "thread_exit" statement or the builtins of the
    \c THREAD_CONTROL functional domain, which is checked when the code is parsed and, for a dynamic call such as
    @ref Qore::call_function() "call_function()", when the call is made.  Module code that the %Program calls keeps its
    own parse options, and it can start threads, including threads that run the %Program's own code:
    for example, a module that calls back its caller from a thread of its own (a poller calling the methods of a
    subclass, a server handler, a WebSocket event callback, a logger appender) runs a closure or a method of the
    restricted %Program in another thread.  A Program with
    @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL" can therefore cause concurrent execution of its code.  A user
    module that the restricted %Program loads first inherits its restrictions and so cannot use
    @ref background "background" (see @ref module_parse_option_masks); module threads come from modules that the host
    loaded already or that are loaded with a parse option mask, which the host gives the %Program as capabilities.

    What the %Program's restrictions do guarantee when module code is involved:
    - the restrictions of the %Program apply to the builtins that module code calls for it, as described in
      @ref user_module_runtime_context; a builtin that runs code asynchronously, such as
      @ref Qore::call_async() "call_async()", @ref Qore::parallel_map() "parallel_map()" or
      @ref Qore::AsyncIoController::addTimer() "AsyncIoController::addTimer()", refuses to run the code of a caller that
      may not control threads (see @ref MODULE_OWNED), but the @ref background "background" operator in module code is
      checked against the module's own parse options only, and module code can always run its own closures, which can
      call the caller's code
    - a thread that module code starts on behalf of a sandboxed caller inherits the caller's
      @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager": its filesystem and network policy, its interrupts, its cancellation
      scope and its thread limit apply to the thread as they apply to the caller (see @ref sandbox_resources)
    - @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setMaxThreads() "SandboxManager::setMaxThreads()" bounds the number of threads started
      on behalf of sandboxed code, through module code or otherwise; a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" has no
      thread limit unless one is set
    - the threads of a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" count against the thread limit of the sandbox of the
      %Program that created the pool (see @ref sandbox_resources), which
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL" does not prevent; a pool created by a %Program without a
      sandbox manager is not limited, and runs each task under the sandbox of the code that submitted it

    To bound the threads of restricted code, give its %Program a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" with a
    thread limit.  A run-time refusal of the @ref background "background" operator in module code for restricted
    callers would break the modules that run their callers' code on their own threads, while the sandbox already
    governs those threads.

    @section sandbox_manager SandboxManager Class

    The @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" class provides fine-grained, configurable sandboxing
    with support for:

    - @ref sandbox_filesystem "Filesystem access control" with allow/deny path lists
    - @ref sandbox_network "Network access control" with IP range and hostname filtering
    - @ref sandbox_resources "resource limits": a thread limit, and a @ref sandbox_resource_limits "wall time limit",
      a @ref sandbox_cpu_limit "CPU time limit" and a @ref sandbox_stack_limit "stack size limit" for each call into
      the sandbox, and a @ref sandbox_memory_limit "memory limit" for the data it creates
    - @ref sandbox_interrupt "Safe code interruption" for stopping runaway code

    @subsection sandbox_manager_usage Basic Usage

    @code{.py}
%new-style
%require-types
%strict-args

# Create and configure sandbox
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setMaxThreads(4);                    # Max 4 threads
sm.blockPrivateNetworks();              # SSRF prevention
sm.setFilesystemDefaultPolicy(False);   # Deny filesystem by default

# Create sandboxed program
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE | PO_STRICT_ARGS);
p.setSandboxManager(sm);

# Parse and run untrusted code
p.parse(user_code, "user_script");
auto result = p.callFunction("main");
    @endcode

    @subsection sandbox_provenance Objects, Callbacks, and Trusted Resources

    An object or runtime call reference created under a sandbox retains that context. Calling its methods or
    invoking the reference later also enforces its creation policy, even when trusted code makes the call and
    the method is implemented in a shared module. The nearest calling Program's policy still applies; an access
    must satisfy both it and every active creation policy. Copies, asynchronous callbacks, and bound callbacks
    preserve this protection. A manager's interrupt and thread limits remain effective as well.

    Creation provenance does not track later changes to a shared object. When a tenant configures an existing
    handler, keep its interface Program wrapper or bind its callback while executing in that interface:

    @code{.py}
code registered_handler = SandboxManager::bindCall(\handler.handleRequest());
    @endcode

    The returned callback retains the registering context without changing the shared handler. Objects and
    references retain their original managers, not their creating Programs. Replacing a Program's manager affects
    future creations; restrict or interrupt the old manager to revoke existing objects and callbacks. Configure
    filesystem/network rules while their users are quiescent; interruption and thread-limit changes are thread-safe.

    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() masks inherited caller policy but cannot remove provenance.
    Trusted infrastructure that must read a system credential or open a system listener registers a fixed operation
    with @ref Qore::SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall() during trusted startup:

    @code{.py}
# Trusted initialization: authenticate_provider uses a system-owned credential path.
code authenticate = SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall(\authenticate_provider());
# Later, while serving a sandboxed caller:
authenticate();
    @endcode

    Only code executing outside every sandbox can register such a capability. Its fixed callback must validate its
    arguments; a capability for an arbitrary file reader grants that operation to everyone holding it. Tenant
    callbacks invoked within the operation regain their own provenance and Program policy. Interrupts and thread
    accounting are preserved. Initialize shared system resources before invoking tenant code; first initialization
    under a tenant intentionally captures that tenant's context.

    Native modules invoke callbacks with ResolvedCallReferenceNode::invoke(), or apply QoreSandboxProvenanceHelper
    around direct native implementations. Foreign runtime I/O that does not use Qore's policy checks remains outside
    this mechanism; provenance is execution context, not data taint tracking.

    @subsection sandbox_module_init Module Initialization

    In this section, code that is not trusted is code that a sandbox governs, and code whose parse options restrict a
    functional domain.

    The directories of library code are the directories of the process-wide module path when the library is
    initialized: those of \c QORE_MODULE_DIR and the standard module directories (or those that the embedding
    application sets before it initializes the library).  A directory added to the process-wide module path later,
    with an unquoted \c %append-module-path directive or by the embedding application, is searched for modules but
    does not hold library code: an application whose modules must be library code puts their directories in
    \c QORE_MODULE_DIR (Qorus, for example, puts \c $OMQ_DIR/qlib and \c $OMQ_DIR/user/modules there; its own
    unquoted directives only repeat them as a fallback).  Code that is not trusted, and a %Program that is sandboxed
    or restricted when it is parsed, cannot extend the process-wide module path: an unquoted
    \c %append-module-path directive is a parse error there (a quoted path adds a directory to the %Program's own
    module path), and the embedding API that adds directories ignores such a caller.  The source of a library module
    can, whoever loads it first: whether such a directive is allowed depends on the source being parsed, not on the
    code that loads it.

    A module found in a directory of library code is library code.  It is loaded from the file that was found there:
    a load by path through a link to a library module loads the library module's file.  Its initialization (the
    static initializers of its classes, its global variable and constant initializers and its \c init closure) runs
    with no sandbox provenance, also when code that is not trusted loads it first: the sandbox of the loading code
    does not govern it, its interrupts and policy do not apply to it, and what it creates (objects, callbacks,
    registrations in the module's own registries, threads it starts) belongs to no sandbox, so a later caller that
    uses it is not affected by the sandbox of the code that happened to load the module.  Every later call into the
    module is governed by its caller, as any other call.  The restrictions of the loading %Program's parse options
    still apply to the module.  The time limits of the loading call still count the time that the initialization
    takes.

    Library code that code that is not trusted loads is not shaped by that code:
    - its source is parsed with the parse defines that the loading %Program's host set (with
      @ref Qore::Program::define() "Program::define()" by trusted code, or by the embedding application), but not
      with those that the loading code defined in its source or set itself; a %Program that code that is not trusted
      creates has no host, so no define of it (its parse option defines included) shapes library code, and a child
      %Program keeps the record of the defines that its parent's host did not set;
    - its dependencies (\c %requires), its optional dependencies (\c %try-module) and its child modules
      (\c %try-child-module) are resolved through its own module paths and the process-wide module path, not through
      the module paths that the loading %Program added, and never to a module that code that is not trusted chose
      (see below), also when that code loaded it before: an optional dependency or a child module is then left out,
      and a dependency is missing;
    - a module that is not library code and that its initialization loads, by path or otherwise, is governed by the
      sandbox of the loading code, and is subject to the same limits as a module that the loading code loads itself
      (see below);
    - code that is not trusted cannot mark imported classes and hashdecls for re-export to the modules that a
      %Program loads (the \c reexport argument of @ref Qore::Program::importClass() "Program::importClass()" and
      @ref Qore::Program::importHashDecl() "Program::importHashDecl()").

    A module that a caller loads by path, or finds through a module path that a %Program added with
    \c %prepend-module-path or \c %append-module-path, is not library code: it is initialized under the sandbox of
    the code that loads it, and a source module's %Program keeps that sandbox.  Neither is a library module that code
    that is not trusted loads into a %Program container of its own (for example with
    @ref Qore::Program::loadApplyToPrivateUserModule() "Program::loadApplyToPrivateUserModule()"): it is parsed and
    initialized with the container's code and parse defines, under the sandbox of the code that loads it.

    Module names are process-wide: once a module is loaded, every later request for its name gets that module.  So
    that code that is not trusted cannot replace a library module, or hand other code a module of its own choosing,
    its loads are limited:
    - it cannot load a module from outside the directories of library code (by path, through a module path that its
      %Program added, or from source) under the name of a module in a directory of library code; the load raises
      \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR, naming the module, the path it was loaded from and the library module that it would
      replace
    - it cannot load a module into a %Program container of its own that is not private (for example with
      @ref Qore::Program::loadApplyToUserModule() "Program::loadApplyToUserModule()") under the name of a module in a
      directory of library code; the load raises \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR, naming the module and the library module
    - a module that it loaded from outside the directories of library code under any other name is given only to code
      that is not trusted and that has the same sandbox, or, for code with no sandbox, that runs in the same %Program:
      a later request for its name from other code, library code included, and a reload raise \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR,
      naming the module and its file

    @section sandbox_filesystem Filesystem Security

    The filesystem security system controls which paths can be accessed by sandboxed code. It supports:

    - <b>Sandbox root</b>: Restrict all access to within a specific directory (chroot-like)
    - <b>Allow/deny lists</b>: Fine-grained path-based access control
    - <b>Access modes</b>: Control read, write, execute, delete, and create permissions separately

    The filesystem policy applies to every file access made for the code: the global filesystem functions such as
    @ref Qore::stat() "stat()", @ref Qore::rename() "rename()" and @ref Qore::mkdir() "mkdir()",
    @ref Qore::File "File" and @ref Qore::Dir "Dir", UNIX domain sockets, @ref Qore::system() "system()" and
    @ref Qore::exec() "exec()", and the file access of modules such as \c i18n, \c json and \c krb5.  Code run with
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() "SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy()" or through a
    capability from @ref Qore::SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall() "SandboxManager::createSystemPolicyCall()"
    does not inherit the sandboxed caller's policy for any of these accesses (see @ref sandbox_provenance for the
    provenance that \c callWithSystemPolicy() still enforces).

    @subsection sandbox_fs_comparison Parse Options vs SandboxManager

    |!Feature|!PO_NO_FILESYSTEM|!SandboxManager
    |Block all filesystem access|Yes, for the %Program's own code|Yes (default deny policy)
    |Applies to the code of modules loaded into the %Program|No|Yes
    |Allow specific directories|No|Yes
    |Deny specific directories|No|Yes
    |Different read/write permissions|No|Yes
    |Sandbox root (chroot-like)|No|Yes

    @subsection sandbox_fs_root Sandbox Root

    Setting a sandbox root restricts all filesystem access to within that directory:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setFilesystemSandboxRoot("/home/sandbox/userdata");

# Now all filesystem operations are restricted to /home/sandbox/userdata
# Attempts to access paths outside will raise FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED
    @endcode

    The sandbox root:
    - Automatically resolves symlinks to prevent escapes, including symbolic links whose target
      does not exist yet
    - Blocks path traversal attacks (../)
    - Uses realpath() for canonical path resolution

    @subsection sandbox_fs_path_resolution Path Resolution

    Every path is resolved to an absolute, symlink-free canonical path before the sandbox rules
    are applied.  Paths that do not exist yet are resolved as well - creating a file, making a
    directory, or checking whether a path exists are all legitimate operations on a path that is
    not there - so the deepest existing ancestor of the path is resolved with realpath() and the
    remaining components are appended, with \c "." and \c ".." folded.

    This means that:
    - the number of missing components in a path does not change the outcome: with a sandbox root
      of \c "/sandbox", a check against \c "/sandbox/missing" and a check against
      \c "/sandbox/a/b/c/d" behave the same way
    - filesystem operations on a path that does not exist report "not found" as they do outside
      a sandbox, instead of raising an access error
    - \c ".." cannot be used to escape the sandbox root through components that do not exist yet
    - a symbolic link pointing outside the sandbox root is denied even when its target does not
      exist, so a dangling link cannot be used to create files outside the root

    A path that cannot be resolved for any other reason - for example because a directory in the
    path cannot be searched - raises \c SANDBOX-PATH-ERROR.  This is a resolution failure, not a
    policy decision, and is deliberately distinct from the \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED raised
    when the sandbox rules deny an access.

    @subsection path_nul_characters Paths With NUL Characters

    An operating system path is a NUL-terminated string, so a string with a NUL character cannot
    name a file: passed to the operating system, it would be cut at the NUL character and name
    another file than the string does, after any check made by %Qore code on the whole string (for
    example a check that the path ends with \c ".txt").  Every %Qore API that passes a string to
    the operating system as a file or directory path, a UNIX domain socket path or socket target, a
    module name or path, or a time zone region or zoneinfo file therefore refuses a string with a
    NUL character, before any sandbox check and before any system call:
    - an API that raises exceptions raises its own error code (for example \c FILE-OPEN2-ERROR
      from @ref Qore::File::open2() "File::open2()"), with \c EINVAL as the exception argument and a
      description giving the string (escaped, with at most its first 64 characters) and the byte
      offset of the NUL character
    - an API that reports errors with its return value and @ref Qore::errno() "errno()" returns its
      failure value (for example @ref False "False" from @ref Qore::is_file() "is_file()",
      @ref nothing from @ref Qore::hstat() "hstat()", or -1 from @ref Qore::unlink() "unlink()"),
      and @ref Qore::errno() "errno()" returns \c EINVAL

    Because the string is refused before the sandbox check, the result is the same with or without
    a sandbox: the error is never a \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED for the path cut at the NUL
    character.

    @code{.py}
string path = "/data/report.csv\x00.txt";
# the string ends with ".txt", but cut at the NUL character it would name "/data/report.csv"
bool b = is_file(path);  # False; errno() returns EINVAL
string text = ReadOnlyFile::readTextFile(path);  # raises FILE-OPEN2-ERROR
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @subsection sandbox_fs_listings Directory Listings and Created Directories

    A listing does not reveal what the sandbox hides: the @ref Qore::Dir "Dir" listing methods
    (@ref Qore::Dir::list() "Dir::list()", @ref Qore::Dir::listInfo() "Dir::listInfo()",
    @ref Qore::Dir::listFiles() "Dir::listFiles()", @ref Qore::Dir::listDirs() "Dir::listDirs()" and
    their variants) and @ref Qore::glob() "glob()" leave out the entries that the filesystem policy
    does not allow to be read, such as an entry under a denied path or a symbolic link to a file
    outside the allowed paths.  A listing of an allowed directory still succeeds; it only shows what
    the program is allowed to read.

    Creating a directory with its parent directories (@ref Qore::mkdir() "mkdir()" and
    @ref Qore::mkdir_ex() "mkdir_ex()" with \a parents, and @ref Qore::Dir::create() "Dir::create()")
    needs \c QSEC_CREATE access for the directory and for each parent directory that would be
    created, so a rule for a directory does not allow its parent directories to be created.

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @subsection sandbox_fs_tz_regions Time Zone Regions

    A time zone region (ex: \c "Europe/Prague") names a zoneinfo file under the zoneinfo directory of
    the system, so a region with a \c ".." or an empty path component or a backslash, which could name
    a file outside of the zoneinfo directory, raises \c TZINFO-ERROR; no region has such a component.
    This applies to @ref Qore::TimeZone::constructor() "TimeZone::constructor()",
    @ref Qore::TimeZone::setRegion() "TimeZone::setRegion()",
    @ref Qore::Program::setTimeZoneRegion() "Program::setTimeZoneRegion()" and to deserialized dates.
    A zoneinfo file given as an absolute path or a path starting with \c "." is a path: it is subject
    to @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "PO_NO_FILESYSTEM", which refuses it without opening the file, and
    to the filesystem policy.

    @since %Qore 3.0

    @subsection sandbox_fs_allow_deny Allow and Deny Lists

    For more granular control, use allow and deny lists:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();

# Allow read access to data directory
sm.addFilesystemAllowedPath("/data/public", QSEC_READ);

# Allow read/write to uploads directory
sm.addFilesystemAllowedPath("/data/uploads", QSEC_READ | QSEC_WRITE | QSEC_CREATE);

# Deny access to secrets (takes precedence over allow)
sm.addFilesystemDeniedPath("/data/uploads/secrets");
    @endcode

    @note Denied paths always take precedence over allowed paths.

    @subsection sandbox_fs_modes Access Mode Constants

    The following constants control filesystem access modes:

    |!Constant|!Value|!Description
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_READ "QSEC_READ"|1|Read access to files
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_WRITE "QSEC_WRITE"|2|Write/modify access to files
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_EXECUTE "QSEC_EXECUTE"|4|Execute access to files
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_DELETE "QSEC_DELETE"|8|Delete files
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_CREATE "QSEC_CREATE"|16|Create new files
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_ALL "QSEC_ALL"|31|All access modes combined

    @subsection sandbox_fs_policy Default Policy

    By default, unlisted paths are denied. You can change this behavior:

    @code{.py}
# Default: deny unlisted paths (most secure)
sm.setFilesystemDefaultPolicy(False);

# Alternative: allow unlisted paths, only deny explicitly listed
sm.setFilesystemDefaultPolicy(True);
    @endcode

    @section sandbox_network Network Security

    The network security system controls socket connections and provides SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery)
    prevention.

    @subsection sandbox_net_comparison Parse Options vs SandboxManager

    |!Feature|!PO_NO_NETWORK|!SandboxManager
    |Block all network access|Yes, for the %Program's own code|Yes (default deny policy)
    |Applies to the code of modules loaded into the %Program, also on I/O threads|No|Yes
    |Allow specific hosts|No|Yes
    |Allow specific IP ranges|No|Yes
    |Block private networks (SSRF)|No|Yes
    |Port restrictions|No|Yes
    |Protocol filtering (TCP/UDP/UNIX)|No|Yes

    @subsection sandbox_net_ssrf SSRF Prevention

    Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attacks trick applications into making requests to internal
    resources. The @ref Qore::SandboxManager::blockPrivateNetworks() "blockPrivateNetworks()" method
    blocks all private and internal network ranges:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();

# Block all private networks (essential for SSRF prevention)
sm.blockPrivateNetworks();

# This blocks:
# - 127.0.0.0/8 (localhost)
# - 10.0.0.0/8 (RFC 1918 Class A)
# - 172.16.0.0/12 (RFC 1918 Class B)
# - 192.168.0.0/16 (RFC 1918 Class C)
# - 169.254.0.0/16 (link-local, includes cloud metadata 169.254.169.254)
# - ::1/128 (IPv6 localhost)
# - fe80::/10 (IPv6 link-local)
# - fc00::/7 (IPv6 unique local)

# Allow public internet access
sm.setNetworkDefaultPolicy(True);
    @endcode

    @note Security checks occur AFTER DNS resolution to prevent attacks using hostnames that
    resolve to internal IP addresses.

    @subsection sandbox_net_hosts Host and IP Restrictions

    Control access by hostname patterns and IP ranges:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();

# Allow specific hosts (supports wildcards)
sm.addNetworkAllowedHost("api.example.com");
sm.addNetworkAllowedHost("*.trusted-domain.org");

# Allow specific IP ranges (CIDR notation)
sm.addNetworkAllowedIPRange("203.0.113.0/24");      # IPv4
sm.addNetworkAllowedIPRange("2001:db8::/32");       # IPv6

# Deny specific ranges (takes precedence over allow)
sm.addNetworkDeniedIPRange("192.168.0.0/16");
    @endcode

    @subsection sandbox_net_ports Port Restrictions

    Control which ports can be connected to:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();

# Allow only HTTPS (convenience method)
sm.allowHTTPSOnly();

# Or configure manually
sm.addNetworkAllowedPort(443, QSEC_NET_TCP);        # HTTPS
sm.addNetworkAllowedPort(80, QSEC_NET_TCP);         # HTTP
sm.addNetworkAllowedPort(53, QSEC_NET_ALL);         # DNS (TCP and UDP)
sm.addNetworkAllowedPortRange(8000, 9000, QSEC_NET_TCP);  # Custom range
    @endcode

    @subsection sandbox_net_rules How Network Access Is Decided

    The network policy is checked for:
    - every address a TCP connection is attempted to, including each address that the host name resolves to
      when several addresses are tried (RFC 8305), connections of @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient", FTP
      control and data connections, and connections made in a @ref Qore::PollPipeline "PollPipeline"
    - the remote address of a QUIC / HTTP/3 connection, including an upgrade to HTTP/3 announced by a server
    - the destination of every UDP datagram sent with @ref Qore::Socket::startPollSendTo() "Socket::startPollSendTo()"
    - every local address bound for TCP, UDP, or a UNIX domain socket
    - the destination of a connection made through an HTTP proxy: the proxy is checked as the address connected
      to, and every address the destination resolves to is checked as well, because the proxy connects to it on
      the client's behalf; a destination that cannot be resolved cannot be checked and is denied
    - every reuse of an open connection that a connection pool or a shared client keeps: the connections of
      @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager "HttpClientConnectionManager" (and of the clients built on it,
      such as @ref RestClientIo::RestClientIo "RestClientIo" and
      @ref WebSocketClient::WebSocketClient "WebSocketClient"), including that of a persistent session, and the
      connection of a @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" object (and of the clients built on it, such as
      @ref RestClient::RestClient "RestClient") are handed only to code whose policy allows where they lead, checked
      as a new connection would be, with the policy as it is at that moment; for a connection through a proxy, both
      the proxy and the destination must be allowed

    The sandbox that governs an operation is the one of the thread that starts it; an operation continued on an
    async I/O thread keeps that sandbox, and so does a task submitted to a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool"
    or with @ref Qore::AsyncIoController::submitTask() "AsyncIoController::submitTask()", such as a listener that
    the @ref httpserverintro "HttpServer" module creates for sandboxed code.  Network checks honor
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy() "SandboxManager::callWithSystemPolicy()".

    A connection is decided by these rules in order, after DNS resolution, for each address:
    -# an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address (<tt>&#58;&#58;ffff:a.b.c.d</tt>) is checked as the IPv4 address it carries, and an
       unspecified destination address (\c 0.0.0.0 or <tt>::</tt>), which the system connects to the local host, is
       checked as the loopback address
    -# an address in a denied IP range is denied, also for an allowed host name, so a name that resolves to a
       blocked network (DNS rebinding) is denied
    -# a port outside the allowed ports for the protocol is denied
    -# a host name that matches an allowed host pattern is allowed
    -# if allowed IP ranges are configured, an address in one of them is allowed and any other address is denied
    -# otherwise the default policy decides

    A bind is decided by the same rules without host names, except that a system-assigned port (port \c 0) is
    not restricted by allowed ports, and denied IP ranges do not deny a bind to a loopback address (\c 127.0.0.0/8
    or <tt>::1</tt>, also as an IPv4-mapped address): a socket bound there can only be reached from the local host, which
    is less than a bind on all interfaces exposes, so a sandbox that blocks private networks, such as with
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::blockPrivateNetworks() "SandboxManager::blockPrivateNetworks()", can still run a
    local listener, while connections to loopback addresses stay denied.  Denied ranges still deny a bind to any
    other address in them, such as a private interface address; allowed ports, allowed IP ranges and the default
    policy apply to loopback binds as to any other bind.  A bind on all interfaces uses the wildcard address, which an allowed IP range
    only allows if it contains the wildcard address itself (for example \c 0.0.0.0/0 or <tt>::/0</tt>); with the default
    deny policy and no allowed IP ranges, no socket can be bound.

    A UNIX domain socket is a file: in addition to the network policy, connecting to one requires read and write
    access to the socket file and binding one requires create and write access under the
    @ref sandbox_filesystem "filesystem policy", so a sandbox without filesystem access cannot reach a local
    service such as a container or database daemon through its socket file.

    Host name patterns are compared without regard to case and without a trailing dot; \c "*.example.com"
    matches \c "example.com" and names with one more label, such as \c "api.example.com", but not
    \c "api.sub.example.com"; \c "*" matches every host name.

    @note Incoming connections accepted by a listening socket, datagrams received, and DNS lookups are not
    controlled by the network policy; use @ref Qore::PO_NO_NETWORK "PO_NO_NETWORK" to disable network access
    completely.

    @subsection sandbox_net_protocols Protocol Constants

    The following constants control network protocols:

    |!Constant|!Value|!Description
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_NET_TCP "QSEC_NET_TCP"|1|TCP protocol
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_NET_UDP "QSEC_NET_UDP"|2|UDP protocol
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_NET_UNIX "QSEC_NET_UNIX"|4|UNIX domain sockets
    |@ref Qore::QSEC_NET_ALL "QSEC_NET_ALL"|7|All protocols

    @section sandbox_resources Resource Limits

    A SandboxManager limits the number of threads that sandboxed code runs at once, which prevents it from
    exhausting the process's threads, the wall-clock time, CPU time and stack size of each call into the sandbox
    (see @ref sandbox_resource_limits, @ref sandbox_cpu_limit and @ref sandbox_stack_limit), and the memory held by the
    data that sandboxed code creates (see @ref sandbox_memory_limit); unlike a parse option, a limit restricts the
    feature rather than disabling it.
    All sandboxed code can also be stopped at any time with
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "requestInterrupt()" (see @ref sandbox_interrupt), and the stack
    used by each thread is bounded as for all %Qore code.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();

# Thread limit - prevents fork bombs
sm.setMaxThreads(4);

# Each call into the sandbox may run for at most 5 seconds
sm.setWallTimeLimit(5000);

# use at most 1 second of CPU time
sm.setCPUTimeLimit(1000);

# and keep at most 10MB of data
sm.setMemoryLimit(10 * 1024 * 1024);

# and use at most 1MB of stack
sm.setMaxStackSize(1024 * 1024);
    @endcode

    Starting a thread beyond the limit raises \c THREAD-CREATION-FAILURE.

    A thread started on behalf of sandboxed code runs under the same sandbox as the code that started it: the
    @ref background "background" operator, @ref Qore::call_async() "call_async()",
    @ref Qore::parallel_map() "parallel_map()", @ref Qore::parallel_foreach() "parallel_foreach()", an
    @ref Qore::AsyncIoController "AsyncIoController" timer callback, and threads that modules start for the caller
    all carry the caller's filesystem and network policy, interrupts, cancellation scope and thread limit.  A thread
    that serves many callers, such as a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" worker or an async I/O thread, is
    governed per task by the sandbox of the code that submitted the task, including the steps of a poll operation
    implemented in %Qore (\c continuePoll() and \c onComplete()), so an asynchronous HTTP request follows a redirect
    or retries a connection only to an address that the sandbox allows.
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setMaxThreads() "SandboxManager::setMaxThreads()" limits the number of such threads
    running at once; starting another one raises \c THREAD-CREATION-FAILURE in the code that starts it, which is module
    code when module code starts the thread for the sandboxed code.  A thread stops counting when its work ends.
    This applies also to a %Program with @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL", whose code cannot
    start threads itself but can cause threads through module code (see @ref sandbox_po_threads).

    The threads of a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" (its own thread, which assigns tasks to workers, and
    its workers) count against the thread limit of the sandbox of the %Program that created the pool, if that
    %Program has a @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager"; the pool keeps that sandbox if the %Program is given
    another one later.  A user module's %Program gets the sandbox of the %Program that loads the module first, so a
    pool that the code of a module first loaded by sandboxed code creates is counted as well.
    - creating a pool when the sandbox has no free thread slot raises \c THREAD-CREATION-FAILURE in the code that
      creates it
    - a pool does not start more workers than the sandbox has free slots: its queued tasks then wait until one of its
      busy workers becomes free, another thread of the sandbox ends or the limit is raised, and a task submitted to
      a pool that has no worker and cannot start one is refused with \c THREAD-CREATION-FAILURE in the code that submits it
    - an idle worker keeps its slot until the pool releases it (see \c max_idle_threads and \c idle_release_timeout
      in @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool::constructor() "ThreadPool::constructor()"); a worker stops counting when it
      leaves the pool, and the pool's own thread when the pool has stopped, before it runs the cancellation code of
      the tasks still queued

    The threads of a pool created by a %Program without a sandbox manager are not counted, as they serve every
    caller; each task runs under the sandbox of the code that submitted it.

    A @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" has no thread limit by default, as no single limit suits every host
    and a default limit would restrict every sandbox that a host creates for other purposes, such as a filesystem
    policy.  Without a limit, sandboxed code is bounded only by the maximum number of threads of the process, which it
    can then exhaust for the host and for other sandboxes.  A host that runs untrusted code should therefore always set
    a thread limit for its sandbox; the presets @ref Qore::SandboxManager::createLockdown() "createLockdown()" and
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::createWebSafe() "createWebSafe()" set one.

    @subsection sandbox_resource_limits Wall Time Limit

    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setWallTimeLimit() "SandboxManager::setWallTimeLimit()" limits the wall-clock time
    of each <b>call</b> into the sandbox.  A call starts when a thread enters code governed by the sandbox from code
    that it does not govern:
    - @ref Qore::Program::run() "Program::run()", @ref Qore::Program::callFunction() "Program::callFunction()",
      a method call on an object of the sandboxed Program, or any other entry into a Program that has the
      sandbox manager
    - a call of a closure, call reference or object method created under the sandbox, made from outside it (for
      example by the host, after the call that created it has returned)

    A thread that is already running a call of the sandbox does not start a new one when it re-enters the sandbox
    (for example when sandboxed code calls trusted code that calls back into the sandbox), so the deadline cannot be
    extended.  Work that the call starts shares its deadline: threads started with the
    @ref background "background" operator, @ref Qore::call_async() "call_async()",
    @ref Qore::parallel_map() "parallel_map()" and @ref Qore::parallel_foreach() "parallel_foreach()",
    @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" tasks, and asynchronous I/O operations and tasks.  With nested
    sandboxes, the deadline of every enclosing call stays in force in the inner sandbox.

    When the deadline passes, every thread of the call raises a \c SANDBOX-TIMEOUT exception (with
    <tt>arg = {"wall_time_limit": </tt><em>ms</em><tt>}</tt>) at its next
    @ref sandbox_interrupt_check_points "check point", and a thread blocked at a check point wakes at once.  The
    exception is raised again at every check point until the thread leaves the call, so sandboxed code cannot catch
    it and go on, and @ref Qore::clear_thread_cancel() "clear_thread_cancel()" does not clear it.  Other calls into
    the same sandbox are not affected, and the caller of the call that timed out continues normally.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setWallTimeLimit(5000);
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.setSandboxManager(sm);
p.parse(untrusted_code, "user_script");

try {
    p.callFunction("main");
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "SANDBOX-TIMEOUT") {
        # main() ran for more than 5 seconds
    }
}
    @endcode

    Changing the limit affects calls that start afterwards.

    @note
    - Native code that does not reach a check point, such as a long regular expression match, a sort of a very
      large list, or a blocking call into a library that is not interruptible, is stopped when it returns, as with
      @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "requestInterrupt()".  A database query that a driver can cancel
      when the sandbox is interrupted is not cancelled by a timeout; the timeout is raised when the query returns.
    - @ref Qore::defer_thread_cancel() "defer_thread_cancel()" defers a timeout like any other cancellation, so that
      cleanup code can finish; do not give sandboxed code the \c THREAD_CONTROL functional domain
      (@ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL") if it must not be able to postpone its timeout.
    - If the resources to track a call's limits cannot be allocated, the call fails with \c SANDBOX-ERROR
      instead of running without them: at its next cancellation point, and, if it has a stack limit, at its next
      call.  The exception names the limits that cannot be enforced, and its \c arg hash has a key for each of them
      as in @ref Qore::SandboxConfigInfo "SandboxConfigInfo" (\c wall_time_limit, \c cpu_time_limit,
      \c max_stack_size, \c memory_limit) with its
      value (no value for a thread that continues such a call, where it is not known).

    @subsection sandbox_cpu_limit CPU Time Limit

    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setCPUTimeLimit() "SandboxManager::setCPUTimeLimit()" limits the CPU time of each
    @ref sandbox_resource_limits "call" into the sandbox: the CPU time used by all threads of the call together,
    including the threads it starts and the thread pool tasks and asynchronous I/O operations that run for it, each
    from the moment it starts working for the call.  Time spent waiting (sleeping, blocked on a lock, a queue or
    I/O) does not count, so a call that mostly waits is bounded by the wall time limit, and a call that computes by
    the CPU time limit.

    When the CPU time is used up, every thread of the call raises a \c SANDBOX-TIMEOUT exception (with
    <tt>arg = {"cpu_time_limit": </tt><em>ms</em><tt>}</tt>) at its next check point, exactly as with the wall time
    limit; the description names the CPU time limit.

    The CPU time is not measured at the check points.  The sandbox watchdog measures it at the earliest point at which
    it could be used up (the remaining time divided by the number of threads of the call, but at least 1 ms apart), so
    a call can use up to about 1 ms of CPU time per thread more than its limit before it is stopped, plus the time to
    its next check point.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setCPUTimeLimit(1000);  # 1 second of CPU time per call
    @endcode

    @note The CPU time limit needs a per-thread CPU clock (\c pthread_getcpuclockid() on Linux and the BSDs, the
    Mach thread API on macOS).  On a platform without one, a call into a sandbox with a CPU time limit fails with
    \c SANDBOX-ERROR instead of running without the limit.

    @subsection sandbox_memory_limit Memory Limit

    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setMemoryLimit() "SandboxManager::setMemoryLimit()" limits the memory held by the
    %Qore values that code governed by the sandbox created and that are still alive: the buffers of strings, lists
    and binary data, the members of hashes and objects, and buffers that binary modules allocate for the code with
    \c q_sb_malloc().  Unlike the other limits, it is not per call: it applies to the sandbox's data as a whole, across
    all calls and threads, and
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::getMemoryUsage() "SandboxManager::getMemoryUsage()" returns the memory charged to it.

    - <b>Data passed out stays charged.</b>  A value that sandboxed code returns or hands to other code is charged to
      the sandbox until it is freed, wherever it is freed, so the sandbox cannot get around its limit by handing data
      to the host.  If other code grows such a value, the growth is charged to the sandbox as well; data that other
      code created and passes to the sandbox is not charged unless the sandbox grows it.
    - <b>Nested sandboxes.</b>  The memory is charged to the sandbox of the call that created it and to every
      enclosing sandbox call with a memory limit, so an inner sandbox cannot use more memory than its caller has.
    - <b>What is not counted.</b>  Memory allocated inside third-party libraries, by modules that do not use
      \c q_sb_malloc(), or by the interpreter itself (code, caches, stacks).

    When the memory in use exceeds the limit, the thread that exceeded it raises a \c SANDBOX-MEMORY-LIMIT exception
    (with <tt>arg = {"memory_limit": </tt><em>bytes</em><tt>}</tt>) at its next
    @ref sandbox_interrupt_check_points "check point", and again at every check point until the memory in use is below
    the limit.  Unlike a timeout, it can therefore be handled: code that catches the exception and frees memory (or
    lets the data that the failed operation created go) can go on.  An allocation itself never fails because of the
    limit; operations that know a large size in advance, such as @ref Qore::strmul() "strmul()" and
    @ref Qore::range() "range()", check it first and raise the exception instead of allocating.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setMemoryLimit(10 * 1024 * 1024);  # 10MB
    @endcode

    @note
    - The limit can be exceeded by at most one operation plus the memory that threads running calls into the sandbox
      have reserved for their next allocations, which is a small part of the limit near the limit.
    - A call charges memory if the sandbox has a memory limit when the call starts; a change of a non-zero limit
      takes effect at once.
    - Hash members are charged with an estimate of their size (the member, its key and its index entries).

    @subsection sandbox_stack_limit Stack Size Limit

    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setMaxStackSize() "SandboxManager::setMaxStackSize()" limits the stack that each
    @ref sandbox_resource_limits "call" into the sandbox may use, which bounds the recursion of sandboxed code.  The
    stack is measured from the position where the call starts; a thread that continues the call, such as a thread
    started with the @ref background "background" operator or a @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" task,
    measures from where it starts the work.  A re-entry into the sandbox does not start a new measurement, so
    sandboxed code cannot gain stack by calling back into itself through trusted code, and with nested sandboxes the
    limit of every enclosing call stays in force.

    Exceeding the limit raises \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED, naming the sandbox limit (with
    <tt>arg = {"max_stack_size": </tt><em>bytes</em><tt>}</tt>), at the next stack check, which is
    made at every function and method call in all execution modes.  The thread's own stack limit still applies: the
    sandbox limit can only make it tighter.  After the call, the thread has its whole stack again.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.setMaxStackSize(1024 * 1024);  # 1MB per call
    @endcode

    @note The stack size limit is only enforced where %Qore checks the stack of its threads, as it does for the
    \c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED exception of all code.

    @section sandbox_interrupt Safe Code Interruption

    The interrupt mechanism allows safely stopping sandboxed code that is stuck in loops
    or blocking operations. This is unique to SandboxManager - parse options cannot stop
    running code.

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE);
p.setSandboxManager(sm);
p.parse(untrusted_code, "user_script");

# Start execution in background thread
Counter done(1);
background sub() {
    try {
        p.callFunction("main");
    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
        # Will catch PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED
    }
    done.dec();
}();

# If execution takes too long, interrupt it
usleep(5s);
if (done.getCount() > 0) {
    sm.requestInterrupt();
}

# Wait for completion
done.waitForZero();

# Clear interrupt for reuse
sm.clearInterrupt();
    @endcode

    @subsection sandbox_interrupt_check_points Interrupt Check Points

    The interrupt mechanism is checked at:
    - <b>Loop boundaries</b>: while, for, foreach, do-while (checked before each iteration)
    - <b>Sleep functions</b>: sleep(), usleep()
    - <b>Lock acquisition</b>: Mutex::lock(), RWLock::readLock(), RWLock::writeLock()
    - <b>Auto-lock constructors</b>: AutoLock, AutoReadLock, AutoWriteLock
    - <b>Queue operations</b>: Queue::get(), Queue::pop()
    - <b>Thread synchronization</b>: Counter::waitForZero(), Condition::wait(), Gate::enter()
    - <b>File I/O</b>: File read, write, and lock operations
    - <b>Stream I/O</b>: PipeInputStream::read(), PipeOutputStream::write()
    - <b>Standard I/O</b>: StdoutOutputStream::write(), StderrOutputStream::write()
    - <b>Database operations</b>: Datasource transaction lock acquisition

    When interrupted, a \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED exception is raised.
    For external process functions (system(), backquote(), and the backquote operator), a SandboxManager also
    starts the child in a new process group so that requestInterrupt() can terminate the entire process group.
    Without a SandboxManager, children remain in the current process group and receive terminal signals (for example,
    Ctrl-C) along with the parent.

    @subsection sandbox_interrupt_polling Waking Blocked Threads

    A thread blocked at a check point (a sleep, a lock or condition wait, or a wait for I/O) is woken as soon as
    @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "requestInterrupt()" is called, so the wait ends at once with
    \c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED; there is no polling interval.  The same mechanism ends the waits of a thread cancelled
    with @ref Qore::cancel_thread() "cancel_thread()"; see @ref cancel_check_points "Cancellation Check Points"
    for the full list of check points.

    @section sandbox_presets Security Presets

    Two convenience presets are available for common use cases:

    @subsection sandbox_lockdown Lockdown Mode

    Maximum security for completely untrusted code:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm = SandboxManager::createLockdown();
# - No filesystem access
# - No network access
# - Wall time limit per call: 5 seconds
# - CPU time limit per call: 1 second
# - Stack size limit per call: 1MB
# - Memory limit: 10MB
# - Max threads: 1
    @endcode

    @subsection sandbox_websafe Web-Safe Mode

    Suitable for web application sandboxing where some network access is needed:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm = SandboxManager::createWebSafe();
# - No filesystem access
# - Network allowed but private networks blocked (SSRF prevention)
# - Wall time limit per call: 30 seconds
# - CPU time limit per call: 10 seconds
# - Stack size limit per call: 2MB
# - Memory limit: 100MB
# - Max threads: 4
    @endcode

    @section sandbox_combining Combining Parse Options and SandboxManager

    Parse options and SandboxManager can be used together for defense in depth:

    @code{.py}
# Use parse options to disable features entirely
Program p(PO_NEW_STYLE | PO_NO_EXTERNAL_PROCESS | PO_NO_DATABASE);

# Use SandboxManager for fine-grained control
SandboxManager sm();
sm.addFilesystemAllowedPath("/data/uploads", QSEC_READ | QSEC_WRITE);
sm.blockPrivateNetworks();
sm.setNetworkDefaultPolicy(True);
sm.setMaxThreads(4);

p.setSandboxManager(sm);
    @endcode

    @section sandbox_configuration Configuration Inspection

    You can inspect the current sandbox configuration:

    @code{.py}
SandboxManager sm();
sm.blockPrivateNetworks();
sm.setMaxThreads(4);

# Get full configuration
hash<auto> config = sm.getConfiguration();
printf("Config: %N\n", config);

# Get filesystem-specific configuration
hash<auto> fs_config = sm.getFilesystemConfiguration();

# Get network-specific configuration
hash<auto> net_config = sm.getNetworkConfiguration();
    @endcode

    @section sandbox_exceptions Security Exceptions

    The following exceptions may be raised by the sandbox system:

    |!Exception|!Source|!Description
    |\c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED|SandboxManager|Filesystem access blocked by security policy
    |\c NETWORK-ACCESS-DENIED|SandboxManager|Network connection blocked by security policy
    |\c PROGRAM-INTERRUPTED|SandboxManager|Execution interrupted via requestInterrupt()
    |\c SANDBOX-TIMEOUT|SandboxManager|The @ref sandbox_resource_limits "wall time limit" or the @ref sandbox_cpu_limit "CPU time limit" of a call was exceeded
    |\c SANDBOX-ERROR|SandboxManager|The limits of a call named in the exception could not be enforced (no resources to track them)
    |\c STACK-LIMIT-EXCEEDED|SandboxManager|The @ref sandbox_stack_limit "stack size limit" of a call was exceeded
    |\c SANDBOX-MEMORY-LIMIT|SandboxManager|The @ref sandbox_memory_limit "memory limit" of the sandbox was exceeded
    |\c SANDBOX-CONFIG-ERROR|SandboxManager|Invalid configuration, such as a negative limit
    |\c THREAD-CREATION-FAILURE|SandboxManager|Thread limit exceeded
    |Parse error|Parse Options|Code uses disabled functionality

    @section sandbox_best_practices Best Practices

    1. <b>Defense in depth</b>: Combine parse options with SandboxManager
       - Use parse options to completely disable unneeded features
       - Use SandboxManager for fine-grained control of allowed features

    2. <b>Always use blockPrivateNetworks()</b> for web-facing applications
       - Essential for SSRF prevention
       - Blocks access to cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254)
       - Blocks access to internal services

    3. <b>Limit threads and stop runaway code</b>
       - Thread limits prevent fork bombs; a SandboxManager has no thread limit unless one is set
       - Set a thread limit also for Programs with @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL", as module
         code that they call can start threads for them (see @ref sandbox_po_threads)
       - @ref Qore::SandboxManager::requestInterrupt() "requestInterrupt()" stops code that runs too long

    4. <b>Use deny-by-default policies</b>
       - Only explicitly allow what is needed
       - Denied paths/IPs take precedence over allowed

    5. <b>Implement interrupt handling</b> for long-running operations
       - Use background threads with timeout monitoring
       - Call requestInterrupt() if execution exceeds expected time

    6. <b>Validate paths before adding to allow lists</b>
       - Use absolute canonical paths
       - Be aware of symlink escapes
       - Test with edge cases

    7. <b>Use lockdown mode for pure computation</b>
       - When code doesn't need external access, use createLockdown()
       - Provides maximum security with minimal configuration

    @section sandbox_security_checklist Security Checklist

    Before deploying sandboxed code execution:

    - [ ] Define the maximum number of threads with
      @ref Qore::SandboxManager::setMaxThreads() "SandboxManager::setMaxThreads()", also for Programs with
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL "PO_NO_THREAD_CONTROL" (there is no limit by default)
    - [ ] Block private networks if any network access is allowed
    - [ ] Use deny-by-default for filesystem access
    - [ ] Implement timeout monitoring with interrupt handling
    - [ ] Disable unneeded features with parse options
    - [ ] Test with malicious input (infinite loops, memory bombs, SSRF attempts)
    - [ ] Log security exceptions for monitoring

    @see @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager"
    @see @ref Qore::Program "Program"
    @see @ref Qore::PO_LOCKDOWN "PO_LOCKDOWN"
    @see @ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS"

    @since %Qore 3.0 (SandboxManager class)
*/
